# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers
# https://opennext.js.org/cloudflare/caching#static-assets-caching
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public,max-age=31536000,immutable

# The civic data layers WardMap.tsx fetches client-side (wards, mayors,
# commissioners, state-legislature, at-large-boundaries, address-index,
# mn-places — see scripts/fetch-*.mjs) — issue #67 Finding 3. Cloudflare's
# own default for static assets is "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
# (always a conditional GET), which is safe but still a network round
# trip on every page load. WardMap.tsx used to override that with `cache:
# "no-store"` (no HTTP caching at all) to guard against a stale cached
# response missing a field the current component code expects — a much
# bigger hammer than the problem needed.
#
# The real fix lives in src/lib/dataUrl.ts's dataUrl() helper (originally
# WardMap.tsx's own, moved out for src/lib/addressChunks.ts's per-county
# address-chunk fetches to share — see issue #70): every fetch is
# cache-busted with a content-hash query param sourced from
# public/data-manifest.json, so a changed file is always a guaranteed
# cache miss regardless of what's cached here. That means these paths
# can now carry a real (moderate, not "immutable" — see below) max-age
# without reintroducing the stale-field risk `no-store` was guarding
# against.
#
# Deliberately NOT `immutable`/a year-long max-age like /_next/static/*
# above: unlike a webpack chunk, the *same* bare path (no query string)
# stays reachable and meaningful on its own — a downstream consumer
# (AGENTS.md §2.4) or the site's own bulk-export link can fetch
# `/wards.geojson` directly, with no `?v=` param, and that response
# needs to go stale on a bounded, short clock rather than sit in a
# browser/edge cache for a year after the next data refresh lands.
/*.geojson
  Cache-Control: public,max-age=600,must-revalidate

/*.json
  Cache-Control: public,max-age=600,must-revalidate

# public/address-index/manifest.json and public/address-index/<county>.json
# (issue #70's chunked gazetteer — see scripts/fetch-addresses.mjs) sit one
# path segment deeper than the two bare globs above. Cloudflare's own glob
# matcher isn't confirmed to treat `/*.json` as recursive into
# subdirectories, so this repeats the same rule explicitly for that one
# path rather than assuming it's already covered.
/address-index/*
  Cache-Control: public,max-age=600,must-revalidate
